The Security Council,

Recalling all its relevant resolutions on the situation in Yemen and the Red Sea, including resolutions 2216 (2015), 2722 (2024), and 2739 (2024),

Condemning in the strongest terms the continued Houthi attacks against merchant and commercial vessels in the Red Sea, the Bab al-Mandeb Strait, and adjacent waters, which threaten the lives of innocent seafarers, jeopardize navigational rights and freedoms, and severely disrupt global commerce and supply chains,

Reaffirming that the exercise of navigational rights and freedoms by merchant and commercial vessels, in accordance with international law, as reflected in the United Nations Convention on the Law of the Sea (UNCLOS), must be respected, and taking note of the right of Member States, in accordance with international law, to defend their vessels from attacks, including those that undermine navigational rights and freedoms,

Reaffirming its respect for the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the coastal States of the Red Sea, and reiterating that States in the region have a leadership role to play, in close cooperation with regional and subregional organizations, in contributing to peace and security,

Noting the vital contributions of international maritime security operations, including the European Union maritime security operation ASPIDES, dedicated to protecting commercial vessels and safeguarding freedom of navigation in the region,

1. Extends the reporting request in paragraph 10 of resolution 2722 (2024) until 15 July 2025;

2. Reiterates its demand that the Houthis immediately cease all attacks against merchant and commercial vessels and immediately release the M/V Galaxy Leader and its crew;

3. Calls for practical cooperation to prevent the Houthis from acquiring the materiel and weaponry necessary to carry out further attacks, and reiterates that all Member States must strictly adhere to their obligations in regard to the targeted arms embargo contained within resolution 2216 (2015);

4. Emphasizes again the need to address the root causes, including the conflicts contributing to regional tensions and the disruption of maritime security, in order to ensure a prompt, efficient, and effective response;

5. Urges caution and restraint to avoid further escalation of the situation in the Red Sea and the broader region, and encourages enhanced diplomatic efforts by all parties to that end, including continued support for dialogue and Yemen’s peace process under United Nations auspices;

6. Decides to remain actively seized of this matter.

